{"task_id": "HumanEval/98", "solution": "def count_upper(s):\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts uppercase vowels at even indices in a string.\n    This function iterates through the input string, checking each character's index and value.\n    \"\"\"\n    count = 0\n    uppercase_vowels = {'A', 'E', 'I', 'O', 'U'}\n\n    for i, char in enumerate(s):\n        if i % 2 == 0 and char in uppercase_vowels:\n            count += 1\n\n    return count"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/99", "solution": "def closest_integer(value):\n    '''\n    Create a function that takes a value (string) representing a number\n    and returns the closest integer to it. If the number is equidistant\n    from two integers, round it away from zero.\n    '''\n\n    num = float(value)\n\n    # Calculate the lower integer (floor equivalent)\n    lower_int = int(num)\n    if num < 0 and (num % 1 != 0):\n        lower_int -= 1\n\n    # Calculate the upper integer (ceil equivalent)\n    upper_int = int(num)\n    if num > 0 and (num % 1 != 0):\n        upper_int += 1\n\n    # Determine distances to the two closest integers\n    dist_to_lower = abs(num - lower_int)\n    dist_to_upper = abs(num - upper_int)\n\n    # Apply rounding logic\n    if dist_to_lower < dist_to_upper:\n        return lower_int\n    elif dist_to_upper < dist_to_lower:\n        return upper_int\n    else: # Equidistant case: round away from zero\n        return upper_int if abs(upper_int) >= abs(lower_int) else lower_int"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/99", "solution": "def closest_integer(value):\n    '''\n    Create a function that takes a value (string) representing a number\n    and returns the closest integer to it. If the number is equidistant\n    from two integers, round it away from zero.\n\n    Examples\n    >>> closest_integer(\"10\")\n    10\n    >>> closest_integer(\"15.3\")\n    15\n\n    Note:\n    Rounding away from zero means that if the given number is equidistant\n    from two integers, the one you should return is the one that is the\n    farthest from zero. For example closest_integer(\"14.5\") should\n    return 15 and closest_integer(\"-14.5\") should return -15.\n    '''\n    f_val = float(value)\n\n    # Calculate the fractional part of the number.\n    # For positive numbers like 3.5, int(3.5) is 3, so fractional_part is 0.5.\n    # For negative numbers like -2.5, int(-2.5) is -3, so fractional_part is 0.5 (-2.5 - (-3)).\n    # This works for both positive and negative numbers.\n    fractional_part = f_val - int(f_val)\n\n    # Check if the number is equidistant from two integers (i.e., its absolute fractional part is 0.5)\n    if abs(fractional_part) == 0.5:\n        if f_val > 0:\n            # For positive X.5, round up (away from zero).\n            # e.g., 14.5 + 0.5 = 15.0 -> 15\n            return int(f_val + 0.5)\n        else:\n            # For negative X.5, round down (away from zero).\n            # e.g., -14.5 - 0.5 = -15.0 -> -15\n            return int(f_val - 0.5)\n    else:\n        # For other values (not X.5), apply standard rounding to the closest integer.\n        # Python's round() handles non-0.5 fractional parts by rounding to the nearest integer.\n        # For example: round(15.3) is 15, round(15.7) is 16.\n        # The int() conversion truncates any remaining decimal after rounding.\n        return int(round(f_val))"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/101", "solution": "import re\ndef words_string(s):\n    \"\"\"\n    You will be given a string of words separated by commas or spaces. Your task is\n    to split the string into words and return an array of the words.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Split the string by one or more spaces or commas.\n    # Then filter out any empty strings that result from consecutive delimiters or leading/trailing delimiters.\n    return [word for word in re.split(r'[ ,]+', s) if word]"}
